                <head>292. Editorial Note</head>
                <p>At 11:30 a.m. on August 5, President Johnson spoke at the dedication ceremonies for a new journalism
                    building at Syracuse University. After reviewing the attacks in the Gulf of
                    Tonkin, he told of the U.S. reprisals and the referral of the question to the
                    United Nations. He then reviewed U.S. policy in Vietnam since 1954 and the
                    aggression of North Vietnam toward its neighbors.</p>
                <p>Later in the day, the President sent a special message to Congress asking for a
                    resolution “expressing the unity and determination of the United States in
                    supporting freedom and in protecting the peace in Southeast Asia.”</p>
                <p>Both the address and the message to Congress are printed in <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Public Papers of the Presidents of the United States:
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Lyndon B. Johnson</hi>, 1963–64,</hi> Book II,
                    pages 928–932, and Department of State <hi rendition="#italic">Bulletin</hi>,
                    August 24, 1964, pages 260–263.</p>
            </div>
